In this lesson we'll look at how to turn a real-world situation into a straight line equation you can actually use. First up, the modelling steps. You start by naming your variable, so something like "let n be the number of items sold" or "let C be the total cost." Then you spot two pieces: a fixed part that stays the same no matter what, like a joining fee, and a rate that changes with your variable, like a price per item. You combine those into the classic y equals m x plus c shape, where c is the fixed bit and m is the rate. Once you've got it, you can plug in a value to predict an outcome, or rearrange to solve for an unknown. Always finish by checking your answer makes sense back in the original story. The most common shape you'll meet is a flat fee plus a per-unit charge, think phone plans or taxi fares. Quick note on profit and loss: profit is when your money in beats your money out, loss is the opposite, and break-even is the exact spot where they're equal. Now let's see it in action.